---
const rustCode = `<code class="language-rust"><span class="token keyword">pub enum</span> <span class="token class-name">NodeState</span> {
    <span class="token class-name">Follower</span>,
    <span class="token class-name">Candidate</span>,
    <span class="token class-name">Leader</span>,
}

<span class="token keyword">pub struct</span> <span class="token class-name">RaftNode</span> {
    <span class="token comment">// Persistent state on all servers</span>
    current_term: <span class="token class-name">u64</span>,
    voted_for: <span class="token class-name">Option</span>&lt;<span class="token class-name">NodeId</span>&gt;,
    log: <span class="token class-name">Vec</span>&lt;<span class="token class-name">LogEntry</span>&gt;,

    <span class="token comment">// Volatile state on all servers</span>
    commit_index: <span class="token class-name">u64</span>,
    last_applied: <span class="token class-name">u64</span>,
    
    <span class="token comment">// Current operational state</span>
    state: <span class="token class-name">NodeState</span>,
}</code>`;
---

<article
  class="max-w-reading mx-auto space-y-8 text-[18px] leading-[1.6] text-text-main"
>
  <p>
    Consensus is the heart of any distributed system. When multiple nodes need
    to agree on a single source of truth—whether it's a database cluster, a
    configuration manager, or a distributed lock service—things get complicated
    quickly. Network partitions, node failures, and message delays conspire to
    create chaos.
  </p>
  <p>
    For years, Paxos was synonymous with distributed consensus. However, Paxos
    is famously difficult to understand and even harder to implement correctly.
    In 2013, Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout introduced <code
      class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
      >Raft</code
    >, an algorithm designed explicitly for understandability.
  </p>
  <h2 class="font-display text-[28px] font-semibold text-text-main mt-16 mb-6">
    The Anatomy of a Node
  </h2>
  <p>
    In Raft, every node in the cluster can be in one of three states: <strong
      class="text-white font-medium">Follower</strong
    >, <strong class="text-white font-medium">Candidate</strong>, or <strong
      class="text-white font-medium">Leader</strong
    >. All nodes start as Followers. If a Follower hears no communication from a
    Leader within a randomized timeout period, it transitions to a Candidate and
    initiates an election.
  </p>
