      />
    </div>
    <figcaption class="mt-4 font-mono text-[13px] text-text-muted text-center">
      FIG 1. Physical manifestation of a distributed network partition.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    When the entry has been safely replicated (meaning a majority of followers
    have acknowledged it), the leader applies the entry to its state machine and
    returns the result of that execution to the client. If followers crash or
    run slowly, or if network packets are lost, the leader retries <code
      class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
      >AppendEntries</code
    > RPCs indefinitely until all followers eventually store all log entries.
  </p>
  <!-- Terminal output block -->
  <div class="relative w-full max-w-code mx-auto -mx-4 sm:mx-auto my-12">
    <div
      class="bg-background-dark border border-border-color p-6 font-mono text-[14px] leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto text-text-muted"
    >
      <pre><code>$ cargo run --bin raft-cluster
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-1] Initializing as Follower (Term: 0)
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-2] Initializing as Follower (Term: 0)
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-3] Initializing as Follower (Term: 0)
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-2] Election timeout reached. Transitioning to Candidate.
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-2] Requesting votes for Term 1...
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-1] Granted vote to NODE-2 for Term 1
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-3] Granted vote to NODE-2 for Term 1
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> <span class="text-text-main font-bold">[NODE-2] Elected Leader for Term 1. Commencing heartbeats.</span></code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Implementing this in Rust provides unique advantages. The ownership model
    ensures that references to log entries are safe across threads, and the type
    system allows us to encode state transitions elegantly using enums and
    pattern matching. In the next section, we'll dive into handling network
    partitions.
  </p>
</article>
